"""Original capital worksheets; synthetic fixtures, static explanations and local arithmetic."""
from education import shell, section_head, E

TOOLS = [
    dict(slug='calculator-jv-debt', kind='jv', title='REIT JV proportionate debt worksheet', description='Separate consolidated borrowings, unconsolidated economic exposure and guarantees without double counting.'),
    dict(slug='calculator-debt-ladder', kind='liquidity', title='Debt maturity ladder & liquidity worksheet', description='Build a dated maturity table and compare it with unrestricted cash and confirmed facility availability.'),
    dict(slug='calculator-preferred-common', kind='preferred', title='Preferred & common distribution bridge', description='Trace a selected period earnings measure to common attribution, keeping preferred distributions and rights separate.'),
]

COPY = {
'jv': [
('The question this worksheet answers', 'How much borrowing is visible when an analyst adds a proportionate share of unconsolidated venture debt to reported consolidated debt? The two views answer different questions. Consolidated statements reflect the accounting perimeter, while a look-through view applies an explicitly chosen economic ownership percentage outside that perimeter. Neither view alone establishes which legal entity must repay a loan. This worksheet deliberately keeps a separate guarantee amount rather than adding every disclosed exposure together.'),
('Formula and synthetic worked example', 'For each unconsolidated venture, multiply gross JV debt by the confirmed economic ownership percentage divided by 100. Add those contributions to consolidated borrowings. The synthetic example is 800 + (500 × 40%) + (400 × 25%) = 1,100. The two venture contributions are 200 and 100. A separate guarantee of 100 remains a separate disclosure; it does not make the result 1,200. An already-consolidated venture contributes nothing extra because its debt is already inside the starting number.'),
('Which ownership perimeter this number uses', 'This worksheet starts with 100% of reported consolidated debt, including debt of consolidated ventures where outside investors may own part of the equity. It then adds only the entered economic share of unconsolidated venture debt. The combined result is consolidated debt plus proportionate unconsolidated JV debt; it is not a fully proportionate parent-only debt figure. The expert JV NAV workshop can instead follow a fully proportionate route, applying the parent economic share consistently to relevant asset values, income and debt, or a full consolidation route with the matching outside ownership adjustment. Those routes require a separate reconciliation. Do not replace this worksheet total with a fully proportionate NAV deduction without reconciling the consolidated minority interests, and do not deduct the same outside claim twice. Neither route changes the underlying legal borrowing or guarantee contracts.'),
('Where to find and reconcile the inputs', 'Read the debt note, investments in unconsolidated entities, consolidation policy and guarantees or commitments disclosures in the same dated filing. Determine whether ownership refers to economic participation, voting rights or a waterfall with differing profit allocations. Match principal with principal or carrying amount with carrying amount; do not silently compare them. Keep currency, monetary scale and as-of date consistent. The notes may disclose only selected venture borrowings, so a known subtotal must not be represented as a complete balance.'),
('How to use the table', 'Enter CSV rows without a header in the order name,debt,ownership,consolidated,asOf. Ownership uses percentage points: 40 means 40%, while 0.40 means 0.40%. The consolidated flag must be true or false. Blank debt or ownership means unknown and makes the complete total unavailable; an explicit zero remains zero. Each row has its own as-of date. Currency, scale and debt basis are selected once for the entire table: convert and document outside this worksheet if the original sources differ. The parser does not infer exchange rates or ownership.'),
('Why no leverage ratio appears', 'A proportional debt numerator does not automatically justify dividing by reported consolidated NOI or EBITDA. The denominator must match the ventures, ownership interests, period and definition used in the numerator. This worksheet does not produce that ratio because it does not collect and reconcile a matching operating measure. It also does not determine recourse, joint liability, distribution restrictions, cross-default exposure or contractual guarantee triggers. Those are separate questions for the actual agreements.'),
('Questions readers ask', 'Does a 40% stake mean a 40% legal payment obligation? No: the proportion is an analytical allocation and contractual obligations can differ. What if the venture is consolidated despite a minority economic stake? Keep it inside the consolidated starting number and mark it already consolidated; do not add its proportional debt again. Can missing ownership be treated as 100% to be conservative? This tool does not make that assumption: it marks the result incomplete. Unknown exposure is information to investigate, not a number to manufacture.')],
'liquidity': [
('The question this worksheet answers', 'What remains after the selected maturity principal is compared with specifically identified liquidity? This is a funding bridge, not a forecast of cash balances. It excludes restricted cash and does not assume new borrowing, renewed facilities, asset sales or future operating cash flow. Its purpose is to make those assumptions visible before an analyst discusses a refinancing requirement. A negative arithmetic residual is not a default probability, a dividend-cut forecast or a legal insolvency conclusion.'),
('Formula and synthetic worked example', 'Available liquidity equals unrestricted cash plus confirmed usable undrawn capacity. Residual after maturities equals that liquidity minus selected-period principal. Subtract separately entered mandatory spending for a second residual. In this synthetic fixture, commitment 100 less draws 60 and letters of credit 10 leaves headroom 30, and confirmed availability is also 30. Cash 40 + availability 30 gives liquidity 70. Restricted cash 15 is excluded. Principal 90 leaves −20; mandatory spending 10 leaves −30. Commitment 100 is not added again.'),
('Locate each input in the filing', 'Start with the debt maturity note, balance-sheet cash notes and MD&A liquidity discussion. Check whether disclosed maturities are principal or carrying amounts and whether the table includes leases, JV borrowings or interest. Look for restricted balances, letters of credit, borrowing-base limits, covenants and facility expiration dates. An undrawn commitment does not prove that the issuer can borrow the same amount under current conditions. Confirm the usable amount independently rather than treating commitment minus draws as automatic availability.'),
('Choose dates before interpreting a ladder', 'Exact dated mode includes maturities after the as-of date through the horizon end, inclusive. Fiscal mode instead includes every supplied fiscal-year bucket and labels the result as fiscal-bucket analysis. It cannot turn year-end totals into exact rolling-next-12-month maturities. In dated mode, the cutoff also tests facility expiry. Fiscal mode instead asks for an explicit resource / facility eligibility date: capacity must remain available through that date, but it does not filter any supplied fiscal-year bucket. This date does not establish that liquidity is available across all supplied years. Fiscal mode therefore withholds complete liquidity and both residuals, even when a facility passes that single-date check. Known debt totals, cash and informational facility amounts remain visible. Reconcile actual dates and use dated mode before calculating a funding bridge. Choose and document it for the source scenario; this is an aggregate comparison, not a cash-timing model. A facility expiring earlier is excluded from confirmed resources even if it could fund some earlier maturities. A more detailed cash-timing model would need individual draw and repayment dates, interest and restrictions; that is outside this page.'),
('Local CSV input and export', 'The editable rows use date,amount,scope,asOf with no header. Import files use that exact header, with YYYY-MM-DD dates in dated mode or four-digit years in fiscal mode. Every row must share the chosen currency, scale, principal basis and source date. The scope text is a label, not a consolidation adjustment: enter final deduplicated principal amounts and reconcile JV shares separately. CSV is limited to 100 KB and 100 maturities. No spreadsheet formulas are executed, no remote URLs are fetched and uploaded files stay inside this browser tab. Export includes your assumptions and full-precision results. Verified decimal maturity amounts are serialized canonically (for example +90 becomes 90), so a valid numeric input can be imported again. Text fields retain spreadsheet-formula escaping.'),
('Questions readers ask', 'Why is availability shown as incomplete? A missing confirmation, an unknown amount or an early facility expiry prevents the tool from claiming a complete funding source. Cash alone remains visible. Why can a fiscal-year view differ from a next-12-month view? The intervals overlap differently, and a year bucket lacks individual dates. Does an unused facility eliminate refinancing risk? No: drawing increases debt and leaves interest, covenants and final maturity to be addressed. The worksheet also ignores intraperiod cash timing and cash that may be needed for normal operations.')],
'preferred': [
('The question this worksheet answers', 'How do preferred and common distributions relate to the earnings measure selected for one matching period? The first step is attribution, not a claim that accounting earnings are cash available to distribute. A measure stated before preferred distributions needs a preferred deduction to describe the common-attributed amount in this simplified bridge. A measure already attributable to common holders must not have the same deduction taken again. The input basis therefore changes the arithmetic visibly.'),
('Formula and synthetic worked example', 'The synthetic fixture uses period earnings of 120, preferred distributions of 10 and common distributions of 90, all USD million for the same period. On a before-preferred basis, common-attributed earnings are 120 − 10 = 110. Total distributions are 10 + 90 = 100. Common-attributed earnings less common distributions are 110 − 90 = 20. If the starting 120 is already common-attributed, it stays 120 and the last difference is 30. The tool does not subtract preferred distributions twice to create 100.'),
('Find the basis before entering numbers', 'Read the income statement attribution, preferred equity note, distribution announcements and the reconciliation for any supplemental measure. A company may show net income attributable to the parent, net income available to common shareholders or a company-specific adjusted amount. Those labels are not interchangeable. Select one measure, preserve its definition and use the same period and currency throughout. Preferred declarations and common declarations must share a declared basis, or both must be paid amounts with their payment timing limitations understood.'),
('What belongs outside this bridge', 'A preferred security may have cumulative distributions, redemption terms, conversion features or restrictions that are not visible in a one-period distribution amount. This worksheet does not build a contractual liquidation waterfall, value the preferred shares or predict recovery on a common claim. It does not infer arrears from one quarter without a distribution, and it does not include debt principal or enterprise value. Those omissions keep an earnings-attribution exercise separate from a legal priority or asset-valuation analysis.'),
('Missing information stays missing', 'Leave an undisclosed distribution blank to mark it unknown. An explicit zero means the user has deliberately entered zero, not that the tool searched and found none. Unknown preferred distributions prevent an unadjusted starting measure from being attributed fully to common holders. An already-common measure can remain usable when preferred distributions are unknown, although total distributions remain unavailable. Negative earnings and negative residuals are preserved as arithmetic; they are not converted into investment ratings or a predicted payment decision.'),
('Questions readers ask', 'Can net income cover a distribution while cash declines? Yes: accounting results and cash movements differ. Can a preferred distribution be deducted from an earnings figure already available to common? That would double count the attribution and is prevented by the selected basis. Does the difference after common distributions prove a dividend is sustainable? No: financing, capital spending, taxes and working capital are not modeled. If the source uses an adjusted measure, preserve the issuer reconciliation and inspect its exclusions before interpreting the bridge.')]
}


def input_field(name,label,value,kind='text'):
    initial_label=' hidden' if name=='resourceDate' else ''
    initial_input=' disabled' if name=='resourceDate' else ''
    return f'<label for="capital-{name}"{initial_label}>{E(label)}<input{initial_input} id="capital-{name}" name="{name}" type="{kind}" value="{E(value)}" maxlength="{500 if name=="earningsDefinition" else 48}"></label>'


def select_field(name,label,options):
    return f'<label for="capital-{name}">{E(label)}<select id="capital-{name}" name="{name}">'+''.join(f'<option value="{E(v)}">{E(t)}</option>' for v,t in options)+'</select></label>'


def page(root,t):
    kind=t['kind']
    fields=input_field('asOf','Common as-of date','2026-09-30','date')+input_field('currency','Currency (three letters)','USD')+select_field('scale','All amounts use one scale',[('million','Millions'),('thousand','Thousands'),('unit','Currency units')])
    if kind!='preferred': fields+=select_field('basis','Debt basis',[('principal','Principal'),('carrying','Carrying amount')])
    if kind=='jv':
        fields+=input_field('consolidatedDebt','Reported consolidated debt','800')+input_field('guarantees','Separate guarantee disclosure (never added)','100')
        rows='JV A,500,40,false,2026-09-30\nJV B,400,25,false,2026-09-30'
        extra=f'<label for="capital-rows">JV rows: name,debt,ownership %,consolidated,asOf<textarea id="capital-rows" name="rows" rows="5" maxlength="100000">{E(rows)}</textarea></label>'
    elif kind=='liquidity':
        fields+=input_field('horizonEnd','Dated maturity cutoff (inclusive)','2027-09-30','date')+input_field('resourceDate','Resource / facility eligibility date (not a maturity cutoff)','2027-09-30','date')+select_field('maturityBasis','Maturity time basis',[('dated','Exact maturity dates'),('fiscal','Fiscal-year buckets (not rolling 12 months)')])+input_field('cash','Unrestricted cash','40')+input_field('restricted','Restricted cash (excluded)','15')+input_field('commitment','Total facility commitment','100')+input_field('drawn','Facility already drawn','60')+input_field('lc','Letters of credit / other deductions','10')+input_field('availability','Confirmed undrawn availability','30')+select_field('conditionsConfirmed','Borrowing-base and covenant availability verified?',[('yes','Yes — synthetic assumption / I checked conditions'),('no','No / unknown')])+input_field('facilityExpiry','Facility expiry','2028-09-30','date')+input_field('mandatory','Mandatory spending for selected maturity set','10')
        extra='<p data-dated-hint class="small-note">Dated mode includes maturities after the as-of date through the inclusive cutoff. The facility must remain available through that cutoff.</p><p data-fiscal-hint class="small-note" hidden>Fiscal mode totals every supplied year bucket, regardless of year. Resource eligibility date checks facility expiry only; it does not cut off maturity rows or prove liquidity through all supplied years. Complete liquidity and residuals are not calculated in fiscal mode because exact timing is unknown; use reconciled exact maturity dates in dated mode for a funding bridge. Mandatory spending must cover the same supplied bucket set.</p><label for="capital-rows">Maturity rows: date,amount,scope,asOf<textarea id="capital-rows" name="rows" rows="5" maxlength="100000">2027-03-31,90,Consolidated principal,2026-09-30</textarea></label><label for="capital-file">Import local maturity CSV (100 KB maximum)<input id="capital-file" type="file" accept=".csv,text/csv" data-maturity-file></label><button class="btn btn-s" type="button" data-maturity-export>Export maturity inputs</button>'
    else:
        fields+=input_field('earningsDefinition','Selected earnings measure and definition','Synthetic selected-period earnings (illustrative)')+input_field('periodStart','Earnings and distributions period start','2026-01-01','date')+input_field('periodEnd','Earnings and distributions period end','2026-09-30','date')+select_field('earningsBasis','Selected earnings attribution',[('before-preferred','Before preferred distributions'),('already-common','Already attributable to common')])+select_field('distributionBasis','Same basis for both distribution classes',[('declared','Declared for the matching period'),('paid','Paid during the matching period')])+input_field('earnings','Selected period earnings measure','120')+input_field('preferred','Preferred distributions','10')+input_field('common','Common distributions','90')
        extra=''
    body=section_head('The capital desk / free worksheet',E(t['title']),t['description'])
    body+='<nav class="wrap breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>→</span><a href="/tools.html">Calculators</a><span>→</span><span>'+E(t['title'])+'</span></nav>'
    body+='<div class="wrap article-meta"><span>By <a href="/about.html">Jeong-Mo Goo · DFB</a></span><span>Published 18 October 2026 · Updated 18 October 2026</span></div>'
    body+=f'<section class="wrap calculator-layout"><form class="calculator-form" data-capital-tool="{kind}" novalidate><fieldset data-calculator-controls disabled><p class="calculator-loading" data-calculator-loading>Calculator controls unlock when the calculation scripts finish loading. If they stay disabled, reload this page or use the formulas and worked example below.</p><h2>Your source and assumptions</h2><p class="small-note"><strong>Synthetic example — not company data.</strong> Edits are unreviewed user scenarios. Blank means unknown; zero must be entered explicitly. All input and imported files stay in this browser tab. Nothing is saved or sent.</p><div class="calculator-fields">{fields}</div>{extra}<p class="small-note">Use one currency, scale and as-of date. Amount fields accept plain decimals, without commas or exponent notation. Rounding happens only for display.</p><div class="btns"><button class="btn btn-p" type="submit">Calculate →</button><button class="btn btn-s" type="reset">Reset example</button></div><p role="alert" class="calculator-error" hidden></p><noscript><p>JavaScript is required for interactive calculations. The formulas and worked example below remain readable.</p></noscript></fieldset></form><div class="calculator-output" role="region" aria-label="Capital worksheet results" aria-live="polite"><h2>Trace the arithmetic.</h2><div data-capital-result><p>No result yet. Calculation requires the controls to finish loading.</p></div><div class="btns"><button type="button" class="btn btn-s" data-capital-export>Export results CSV</button><button type="button" class="btn btn-s" data-capital-print>Print worksheet</button></div></div></section>'
    body+='<article class="wrap policy-reading lesson-body">'+''.join(f'<section><h2>{E(title)}</h2><p>{E(text)}</p></section>' for title,text in COPY[kind])
    body+='<section><h2>Primary sources, scope and corrections</h2><p>These sources explain filing structure and security claims; they are not sources for the synthetic numbers above. DFB wrote the example and formulas for education. No issuer dataset or current market data is loaded.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.sec.gov/answers/reada10k.htm">SEC: How to Read a 10-K — financial statements, notes and liquidity discussion</a></li><li><a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks">Investor.gov: Stocks — common and preferred shareholder claims</a></li></ul><p>Related reading: <a href="/learn-expert-jv-nav.html">JV ownership and valuation</a> · <a href="/learn-debt-maturities.html">Debt maturity analysis</a> · <a href="/learn-expert-refinancing-model.html">Refinancing modeling</a> · <a href="/learn-payout-ratio.html">Distribution denominators</a>.</p><p class="small-note">Educational scenarios, not forecasts or personalized investment advice. <a href="/editorial-policy.html">Editorial standards</a> · <a href="mailto:dividendforensics@gmail.com">Report an error with its page and source</a>. Formula version capital-1.0.2. No rating or safety threshold is assigned.</p></section></article>'
    return shell(root,t['slug']+'.html',t['title'],t['description'],body,extra='<script src="/vendor/decimal.js/decimal.js" defer></script><script src="/capital-calculators.js?v=capital-1.0.2" defer></script>')


def outputs(root):
    return {root/(t['slug']+'.html'):page(root,t) for t in TOOLS}
